k contract, and was bounded (single reboot, immediately re-verified). One minor residue: the reboot was scheduled via 'nohup sh -c ... >/tmp/reboot.log 2>&1', and /tmp/reboot.log persists in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot without being cleaned up afterward — a low-risk logging artifact rather than a security or functional issue. No failed services, no package-health regressions, and no unexpected listening sockets beyond expected chronyd/sshd PID churn from the reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-826e2d67b80b487c\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The core task — running os-comparison-worker as a systemd service 'inventory-worker' under a dedicated 'inventorysvc' system account on node1 — is fully satisfied with direct evidence of account creation, correct unit configuration, and active/enabled/running state confirmed both before and after a full node reboot. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also executed cleanly: healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id bf8b9d80..., service active), a restart acknowledgement, a confirmed new boot identity (218798de...) cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and full recovery of the service under the correct account post-reboot. The only hygiene concern is a leftover /tmp/reboot.log artifact from the reboot-scheduling command, which is minor and low-risk.\",\"limitations\":[\"The public task contract does not explicitly require reboot persistence, so the restart evidence is assessed only as the independent provider-wide protocol, not as a scored material outcome.\",\"No evidence was captured of long-running stability of the service beyond a few seconds after each start/reboot, so behavior over extended uptime is not directly observed.\",\"/tmp/reboot.log was left on disk after the reboot; its exact content was not captured, only its presence in the temporary-files